{"title":"Lebesgue measure","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>The <b>Lebesgue measure</b> is a method of categorizing sets by magnitude devised by French analyst <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Henri_Lebesgue&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Henri Lebesgue (page does not exist)\">Henri Lebesgue</a> for his doctoral thesis in the early 20th century.\n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Lebesgue_measure&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018002744\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.006 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.010 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 7/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 289/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 0/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.605      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.605      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:7903:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018002744 and revision id 31444. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}